* A program transformer written in Refal -- objective T-1.
*
* T-1 asks for a transformer that is not itself a compiler, and the metacode of
* Chapter 6 is what makes one expressible: a program is data, so a Refal
* function can take one apart and put a different one back.
*
* The shape follows Turchin's own section 1.3 example, which replaces the symbol
* '+' by '-' "on all levels of parenthesis structure". That example transforms
* an expression; this one transforms a program -- the input is a metacoded
* program, the rewrite runs at every level of bracket nesting, and Up lifts the
* result back out of metacode.
*
* In this dialect the marker for the object asterisk is the two-term sequence
* `*` `V`, so the program below carries one deliberately: it shows the
* transformer walking metacode rather than plain syntax, and it shows Up
* collapsing the marker back to a bare asterisk on the way out.

$EXTERNAL Dn, Prout, Up;

$ENTRY Go {
  = <Prout <Up <Rename Plus Minus <Dn (Plus '*' (Plus 'a'))>>>>;
}

* Replace every occurrence of one symbol by another, at every bracket level.
* The recursion is structural: a bracket is rebuilt around its rewritten
* contents, and the marker terms are ordinary symbols that simply fail to
* match, which is what keeps the metacode intact.
Rename {
  s.Old s.New = ;
  s.Old s.New s.Old e.Rest = s.New <Rename s.Old s.New e.Rest>;
  s.Old s.New (e.Inner) e.Rest =
      (<Rename s.Old s.New e.Inner>) <Rename s.Old s.New e.Rest>;
  s.Old s.New s.Other e.Rest = s.Other <Rename s.Old s.New e.Rest>;
}
